    .EncartaHistory = "Before the arrival of Europeans, the lands of Brazil were home to many Indigenous peoples, such as the Tupi, the Guarani and the Tapuia. In 1500, the Portuguese fleet of Pedro \xC3\x81lvares Cabral reached the coast of Bahia, and the territory became a Portuguese colony built on brazilwood, sugar cane and, later, the gold of Minas Gerais, sustained for centuries by the forced labor of enslaved Africans. In 1808, fleeing Napoleon, the Portuguese royal family moved to Rio de Janeiro. In 1822, Prince Pedro declared independence and became Emperor Pedro I. The Empire lasted until 1889, one year after the abolition of slavery, when the republic was proclaimed. In the 20th century, Brazil went through the Vargas Era, industrialization, the construction of Bras\xC3\xADlia, a military dictatorship and redemocratization in 1985, becoming one of the largest economies in the world.",
